ent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, but can be grumpy, uncooperative. Candidate A: quick correct decisions, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership.\n\nWe need to discuss. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate A's reliability and positive crew atmosphere outweigh communication difficulty? Or we can raise concerns about Candidate B's uncooperative behavior. Or we can bring up Candidate C's leadership and anticipation. Let's decide.\n\nGiven the criteria, reliability is crucial. Candidate A is 100% reliable. Candidate B is very conscientious (reliability). Candidate C unknown reliability. Candidate D unknown reliability.\n\nStress resilience: B handles stress well. D responds adequately. C unknown. A unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: B good at weather, computer. C sees complex connections, spatial vision. D solves problems extremely well. A quick decisions.\n\nLeadership: C very good leadership. D weak leadership. A unknown. B unknown.\n\nCrew cooperation: A creates positive atmosphere, but difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric. B can be grumpy, uncooperative. C unfriendly. D arrogant, moody, loner.\n\nThus maybe Candidate A is strong on reliability and crew atmosphere, but communication issues. Candidate B strong on stress and technical but crew cooperation issues. Which is more critical? Crew cooperation is essential. A's difficulty communicating ideas may be less severe than B's uncooperative attitude? Hard to say.\n\nWe could propose Candidate A as a better fit due to reliability and positive atmosphere,